"""Builds pilots/L12/job.json — The Radium Girls (long-form, ~7-8 min).
Sources: Damn Interesting 'Undark and the Radium Girls'; WorldAtlas 'Who Were the Radium Girls?'; Kate Moore, The Radium Girls (2017) as summarised by Smithsonian/NPR; Wikipedia 'Radium Girls' + citations."""
import json, pathlib, re
CH = [
 ("Lip, dip, paint", """[serious] In the late 1910s, in a factory in Orange, New Jersey, young women were told to put radioactive paint in their mouths. Hundreds of times a day. And they were told it was safe. Within a few years, their teeth were falling out and their jaws were crumbling. Some of them glowed in the dark. Their bosses said it was something else. Their doctors were paid to agree. And then a small group of dying women decided to sue one of the most powerful industries in America. By the end of this file, you'll see the report the company hid, the bones that gave the secret away, and the courtroom where the women were too sick to raise their hands."""),
 ("The miracle element", """Radium was discovered by Marie and Pierre Curie in 1898. It glowed. It gave off energy, apparently forever. And in the early 1900s, the public went wild for it. It was sold in tonics, in face creams, in toothpaste, and in water you could drink for your health. Doctors used it to treat cancer, and many people believed that a little radium was good for you. One of its most popular uses was luminous paint. Mixed with zinc sulfide, a tiny amount of radium made a paint that glowed in the dark. The U.S. Radium Corporation sold it under the name Undark. And when America entered the First World War, the military needed thousands of watches and instrument dials that soldiers could read at night. So the company hired young women, many of them teenagers, to paint the numbers by hand."""),
 ("The dial painters", """The work paid well, by the piece, and it was considered a glamorous job. The brushes were made of fine camel hair, and the numbers were tiny. To keep a sharp point, the women were taught a technique. Lip, dip, paint. Shape the brush between your lips, dip it in the radium paint, paint a number, and repeat. Over and over, all day long. They swallowed small amounts of radium every day. They were told it was harmless. Some of them painted their nails and teeth with it for fun, and went home glowing. Dust from the paint covered their hair and clothes. Meanwhile, in the same company, the chemists who handled radium used lead screens, masks and tongs. Radium, it turned out, acts a lot like calcium in the body. Once swallowed, it settles into the bones. And there, it keeps on giving off radiation, day after day, for years."""),
 ("Radium jaw", """Around 1922, the first painters started getting sick. Teeth fell out. Sores wouldn't heal. Jawbones began to crumble and break apart. A former painter named Mollie Maggia died that year. Her death was blamed on syphilis. A dentist in New York, who saw several of the women, began to suspect their work, and called it radium jaw. The company commissioned a study by experts from Harvard. In 1924, they reported that the paint was very likely the cause. According to the historians who later studied the case, the company's president did not share that conclusion. Instead, a report was given to state officials that said the workers were in good health. Then, in 1925, the medical examiner of Newark, Harrison Martland, developed tests that could detect radioactivity in a living person's breath. He found it in the painters. And he concluded that the radium inside them was what was destroying their bodies. In 1927, Mollie Maggia's body was exhumed. Her bones were radioactive. It had never been syphilis."""),
 ("The trial", """By then, a former painter named Grace Fryer had been looking for a lawyer for two years. Most were afraid to take on the radium industry. Finally, in 1927, a young lawyer named Raymond Berry agreed. Four other women joined her: Edna Hussman, Katherine Schaub, and two sisters of Mollie Maggia, Quinta McDonald and Albina Larice. The newspapers called them the Radium Girls, and the story spread around the world. The company's lawyers tried to delay, knowing the women might not live long enough to win. At one hearing, some of the women were too sick to raise their arms to take the oath. In 1928, the company settled. Ten thousand dollars each, a yearly payment of six hundred dollars, and their medical costs. Within a few years, all five were dead. But it wasn't over. In Ottawa, Illinois, another company, Radium Dial, had used the same methods. In 1938, a dying former painter, Catherine Donohue, won her case. Part of the hearing was held around her bed."""),
 ("What the file tells us", """The Radium Girls changed American law. Their cases helped establish the right of workers to sue their employers for diseases caused by their jobs. And when scientists built the atomic bomb in the 1940s, they used what had been learned from the dial painters to set safety limits for radiation. Researchers studied the surviving women for decades. In a way, they protected the workers who came after them. So here is what the file tells us. The danger wasn't hidden from everyone. The chemists protected themselves. The company had a report that named the cause. What the painters didn't have was the truth, until they fought for it. If you had been told your job was safe, how long would it take you to believe otherwise? Tell me in the comments. This is Dossier Decoded."""),
]
